---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const keywords =
  'online compass, online compass tool, free online compass, online compass free, compass online, compass direction, live compass, mobile compass, compass tool, digital compass, directions compass, online compass direction, online compass to find north, compass to find north, where is north, north direction, north, south, east, west, direction, online tool, online tool compass';

const title = 'How to Find North Without a Compass (And With Your Phone)';
const description =
  'Find north in seconds with an online compass or with no tools at all. Step-by-step sensor setup, degree readings and sun or shadow checks that confirm it.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} keywords={keywords} type="article">
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a
      href={translatePath('/onlinecompass/blog/')}
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Compass Blog</a
    >
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">
      How to Find North Without a Compass (And With Your Phone)
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-8">November 7, 2026</p>

    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      “Where is north?” is easy to answer until you are standing in a hotel corridor, a basement or a
      street you have never walked. Your sense of direction gives up after two turns, and the answer
      you need is a number, not a feeling.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      This guide takes you from a blank browser tab to a heading you can trust, then shows you how to
      confirm it without any tools at all. It covers sensor setup on each kind of device, how to read a
      <strong>compass direction</strong>, two worked examples, and what to do when the reading looks
      wrong. It uses the <strong>online compass</strong> on this site, but most of the steps apply to
      any <strong>digital compass</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Before you start: two things that ruin a reading
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Check both of these before you calibrate anything. Each one quietly spoils the heading.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Move away from metal.</strong> A car roof, a steel door, a desk and a speaker all bend the local magnetic field. Two metres of clear space is usually enough.</li>
      <li><strong>Take the phone out of a magnetic case.</strong> Magnetic closures and magnetic car mounts are the most common cause of a wrong <strong>north direction</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 1: Open an online compass and allow the sensor
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Open the <strong>online compass tool</strong> and tap allow when your browser asks for motion and
      orientation permission. This is the only permission the page needs, and it is asked once per
      session.
    </p>
    <h3 class="font-sans text-base sm:text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-8 mb-2">
      On a phone or tablet
    </h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A <strong>mobile compass</strong> is the easiest to use, because the magnetometer sits in the
      device you are already holding. Open the page, allow the prompt, and the dial starts moving with
      you. Nothing needs installing, which is why this is a <strong>free online compass</strong> you can
      use on a friend’s phone without asking them to install anything.
    </p>
    <h3 class="font-sans text-base sm:text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-8 mb-2">
      On a laptop or desktop
    </h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Most laptops have no magnetometer, so a live heading will not appear. The page still works as an
      <strong>online tool</strong> for reference, but for a real reading reach for a phone. If your
      laptop does have a sensor and the browser blocks orientation events, try a different browser
      before giving up on it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 2: Hold the device flat and let it settle
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Hold the device flat at roughly waist height and stand still for two seconds. A
      <strong>live compass</strong> filters its output to stop the dial shaking, so a fast spin leaves
      the reading behind for a moment. Let it catch up before you trust the number.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      If the needle swings in circles, you are standing near interference. Step away, hold still, and
      watch the <strong>compass online</strong> settle. If it still drifts, rotate the device in a slow
      figure-eight four or five times to recalibrate the sensor.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 3: Read the online compass direction
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The reading has two parts, and both are useful. The number tells you the
      <strong>online compass direction</strong> in degrees clockwise from north. The name tells you the
      same thing in words.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>0°</strong> — north</li>
      <li><strong>90°</strong> — east</li>
      <li><strong>180°</strong> — south</li>
      <li><strong>270°</strong> — west</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A value near 0° or 360° means you are facing north. Turn slowly and watch the degrees climb: that
      is how a <strong>directions compass</strong> tells you which way to rotate to face the
      <strong>direction</strong> you actually want, rather than only naming it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 4: Confirm it without any tools
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A second opinion costs nothing and catches a bad reading immediately, which matters when the whole day depends on the first one being right.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Use the sun.</strong> It rises in the east and sets in the west, so in the morning your shadow points west and in the afternoon it points east.</li>
      <li><strong>Use midday.</strong> In the northern hemisphere the sun sits roughly south around noon.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the stars.</strong> At night the North Star sits almost over true north.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      These methods give a rough answer only, but that is exactly what you need to check a
      <strong>compass tool</strong> against. When the reading and the sun agree, the instrument is right
      and you can act on the number.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 5: Know when a physical compass wins
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A phone is the fastest option, but it is not always the right one. A baseplate compass has no
      battery, no screen and no software, so it survives cold, rain, a week in a pack and a drop on
      rock. If you are walking a long route with a paper map, carry both and treat the phone as the
      quick check.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      There is also an accuracy trade-off worth knowing. A good 60 mm baseplate can detect a slope a
      phone sensor will round away, while a <strong>mobile compass</strong> recovers faster after you
      walk past a steel door. Neither instrument is better at everything, so use the
      <strong>free online compass</strong> for quick bearings and a needle when the answer has to hold
      for hours.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Worked examples
    </h2>
    <h3 class="font-sans text-base sm:text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-8 mb-2">
      Example 1: Finding north in a hotel room
    </h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      You want to know which wall gets the morning light.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>Step away from the TV, the minibar and the door frame.</li>
      <li>Open the <strong>online compass to find north</strong> and allow the sensor.</li>
      <li>Hold the phone flat, face the window wall, and read the degrees.</li>
      <li>A reading of 85° means that wall faces east, so the morning sun arrives there.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3 class="font-sans text-base sm:text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-8 mb-2">
      Example 2: Checking a photo setup
    </h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      You are shooting into a doorway and want to know if the camera faces the sun.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>Hold the phone flat beside the camera, not in front of the lens.</li>
      <li>Read the heading and note it down.</li>
      <li>If it reads near 270°, you are pointing west, which suits a sunset shot.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Troubleshooting
    </h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-canvas-elevated">
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Problem</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Likely cause</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Fix</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">No heading appears</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">No magnetometer, or blocked permission</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Allow the prompt, then try another browser</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Dial spins in circles</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Metal or a magnet nearby</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Move away and let it settle</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Always off by the same amount</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Sensor needs calibrating</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Figure-eight motion, then re-read</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Move away from metal, allow the sensor, hold the phone flat, and read the number. Confirm it with
      the sun or a shadow. If you want a <strong>compass to find north</strong> that works offline once
      loaded, needs no account, and keeps every reading on your device, this
      <strong>online compass free</strong> tool is one tap away — and the same
      <strong>online tool compass</strong> answers <strong>where is north</strong> in any room, street
      or building you walk into.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
